      {
        "id": 201433,
        "name": "Eric R",
        "username": "Eric_R",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/b9bd4f/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2017-08-08T01:12:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"dpt\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dpt/48/1922_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> dpt:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It is number 1. What wording would remove the ambiguity? Remember we also want the Invaders to possibly deal damage in different lands.(I believe it's also intended that a single City could kill three Explorers in three different adjacent lands, i.e., splitting up its damage.)</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Yes. As a general rule, whenever the rules say \"X deals damage to Y\", it is simply specifying what you're allowed to assign the damage to; it does NOT mean \"to each Y\". <em>Eg: \"5 Damage to [Town]/[City]\" = \"5 Damage allocated as desired among Towns and/or Cities in target land\", NOT \"5 Damage to each Town/City in target land\".</em></p>\n<p>Anything which does damage to each-of-a-thing will explicitly use the word \"each\" (or \"every\", or \"all\") - \"to each Invader\", \"in every adjacent land\", \"destroy all Invaders\", etc.</p>",
        "post_number": 4,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:29:56.203Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "incoming_link_count": 8,
        "reads": 0,
        "readers_count": 0,
        "score": 40.0,
        "yours": false,
        "topic_id": 17000,
        "topic_slug": "instruments-of-their-own-ruin-question",
        "display_username": "Eric R",
        "primary_group_name": "playtesters",
        "flair_name": "playtesters",
        "flair_url": "/uploads/default/original/1X/f46928191dbf93b4d0df24571543f49779579225.png",
        "flair_bg_color": "",
        "flair_color": "",
        "flair_group_id": 41,
        "badges_granted": [],
        "version": 1,
        "can_edit": false,
